WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:15.969
[guitar music playing]

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:16.969
[pause]

00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:20.289
&gt;&gt;presenter: It's my very great pleasure today to welcome

00:00:20.309 --> 00:00:23.640
today's View from the Top speaker, Ted Turner. It's

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.250
difficult to know where to begin when introducing someone

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:30.679
like Ted Turner because he has made an impact in so many

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:32.339
different areas.

00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:35.359
He's primarily known in the business world for his impact on

00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:38.179
the media, which began when he took over a billboard

00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:42.459
advertising business when he was 24 years old. He used

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:46.059
profits from this enterprise to enter cable television just

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:50.279
as it was beginning to take hold in the US. In what was an

00:00:50.299 --> 00:00:54.929
audacious move at the time, he launched a 24-hour cable news

00:00:54.949 --> 00:00:57.129
network in 1980.

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.219
CNN was the first all news network TV in the US. At the

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:04.930
time, many questioned how he could fill the airwaves with

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.840
news content continuously. Today, it is difficult to imagine

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:13.010
what news and politics was like before 24-hour coverage

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.030
existed.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.480
After launching CNN, Ted began buying media content,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.909
including the film studio, MGM, and used the library to

00:01:22.929 --> 00:01:27.959
create multiple, successful franchises, including TNT and

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:31.680
Turner Classic Movies. The resulting Turner Broadcasting

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.859
Systems eventually merged with Time Warner in 1996. Mr.

00:01:37.879 --> 00:01:40.670
Turner was Vice Chairman of Time Warner and was its largest

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:46.099
stockholder when the company merged with AOL in 2000, in

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:49.599
what I'm sure Mr. Turner would agree with perfect hindsight,

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:54.689
was probably not optimal timing. But, in many respects, Ted

00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:57.030
is not here to talk about business at all.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:00.049
He's on campus to attend a meeting of the United Nations

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:03.609
Foundation where he is Chair of the Board. The UN Foundation

00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:07.219
was established with Ted's pledge of one billion dollars in

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:12.359
1998; the largest philanthropic gift of its time. The

00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:15.089
foundation was established to strengthen and support the UN

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:20.089
and its causes through a blend of advocacy, grant making,

00:02:20.109 --> 00:02:21.729
and partnerships.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:26.169
These causes include children's health, climate and energy,

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:31.539
sustainable development, women and population initiatives.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.079
Ted is also an outspoken environmentalist, and as the

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:38.589
largest land owner in the United States with more than two

00:02:38.609 --> 00:02:42.549
million acres of land, an area larger than the states of

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:45.019
Delaware and Rhode Island combined--

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:46.039
[laughter]

00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:49.429
his decision to focus on ecological sustainability in land

00:02:49.449 --> 00:02:54.349
management has already had a major impact. Ted is Atlanta

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:59.419
Braves baseball team and Atlanta Hawks basketball team.

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:03.109
Not content to just stand on the sidelines, however, he also

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:09.249
was skipper of America's Cup Sailing Team in 1977. He's

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:12.919
known to be outspoken and controversial, earning him the

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:17.999
nickname, "The Mouth from the South." But, he has also been

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:21.509
named other things, such as Man of the Year by Time Magazine

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.389
and Humanist of the Year by the American Humanist

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:26.219
Association.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:29.719
While this is only a partial list of accomplishments, one

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:32.839
thing that ought to be abundantly clear is that when Ted

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:36.859
Turner does something, he does not do it meekly. It should

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:39.619
make for an interesting discussion today.

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:42.609
Ted is going to be interviewed by Jason LeeKeenan, one of

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:46.739
our second year leaders of the Speaker's Series, before

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:50.819
opening the discussion to Q&amp;A. Jason also has a record in

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.089
the media business, having worked for Hulu during the summer

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:57.309
and for Disney prior to business school, as well as Goldman

00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:01.959
Sachs and Texas Pacific Group. I'm not sure how Jason is at

00:04:01.979 --> 00:04:06.019
sailing, but he's an impressive classical pianist. Please

00:04:06.039 --> 00:04:09.619
join me today in welcoming Ted Turner to the Stanford

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:10.959
Graduate School of Business.

00:04:10.979 --> 00:04:11.979
[long applause]

00:04:10.979 --> 00:04:33.069
&gt;&gt;Jason: Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you again Mr.

00:04:33.089 --> 00:04:34.089
Turner for coming --

00:04:34.089 --> 00:04:35.089
&gt;&gt;Ted: Ted.

00:04:34.589 --> 00:04:37.839
&gt;&gt;Jason: to see us today. Sorry, I'll call you Ted. That is

00:04:37.589 --> 00:04:39.089
the name of your book, right?

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:39.589
&gt;&gt;Ted: Right.

00:04:39.589 --> 00:04:40.589
[laughter]

00:04:39.589 --> 00:04:42.589
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, I thought I'd start off asking you a question

00:04:42.339 --> 00:04:44.780
on entrepreneurship. As you probably know, Stanford is well-

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.399
known for inspiring entrepreneurship amongst its students

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:51.669
and I'm sure many of the students in the audience today one

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:54.419
day would like to start their own company. As the founder of

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.970
many companies, including CNN, TBS, Ted's Montana Grill, and

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.110
Turner Renewable Energy, could you talk to us a little bit

00:05:03.129 --> 00:05:05.379
about the challenges you faced in starting new businesses?

00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:11.870
&gt;&gt;Ted: It's a lot of work. It's a lot of work and you've got

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:22.030
to have a, a good concept of what you wanna do and, and you

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:28.170
need to have some knowledge of how to obtain capital because

00:05:28.189 --> 00:05:36.600
any business of any size requires capital. And then, I'd

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.620
hate to say it, but you can't, you can't do it in, in, in

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:43.839
just a couple of lines. When I get asked that question,

00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:47.639
though, when I get asked at groups like this I say, you

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:50.459
know, "The secret of success in business is 'early to bed,

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:52.920
early to rise, work like hell and advertise'."

00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:53.939
[laughter]

00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:59.620
You know, that's about as good as, as anything. But, but,

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:03.490
but I would recommend, because in a book you can cover it

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:05.509
better. That's why I wrote the book.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:06.259
[laughter]

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:10.030
&gt;&gt;Jason: This sounds like advertising to me.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.550
&gt;&gt;Ted: And also, I don't have to say the same, the same damn

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.050
thing over and over again, you know?

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.050
[laughter]

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.949
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, lemme, lemme ask--

00:06:16.969 --> 00:06:20.219
&gt;&gt;Ted: One of the things we did a lot in television, we had

00:06:20.219 --> 00:06:21.469
a lot of reruns and--

00:06:21.469 --> 00:06:22.469
[laughter]

00:06:21.469 --> 00:06:24.469
and when you get over 70, life just turns into one big

00:06:23.719 --> 00:06:24.719
rerun.

00:06:24.719 --> 00:06:25.719
[laughter]

00:06:24.719 --> 00:06:29.639
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, you've founded these companies over a period of

00:06:29.659 --> 00:06:31.360
30 years, have the leadership challenges--

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:32.639
&gt;&gt;Ted: Fifty years.

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:34.659
&gt;&gt;Jason: Fifty years. Have the leadership challenges changed

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:35.409
over that time?

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:42.100
&gt;&gt;Ted: No, not really, I don't think. It's still pretty much

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.920
the same thing. Don't forget to advertise.

00:06:48.939 --> 00:06:49.939
[laughter]

00:06:48.939 --> 00:06:51.329
&gt;&gt;Jason: Shifting gears then for a sec--

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:52.349
[laughter]

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:55.720
So, in, in 1998, you founded the UN Foundation with an

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.420
historic one billion dollar donation. What motivated you to

00:06:59.439 --> 00:07:01.439
make this donation and why the United Nations?

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:04.430
&gt;&gt;Ted: The UN was in lot of trouble. They were in financial

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:10.839
trouble and this was back before governments bailed out

00:07:10.859 --> 00:07:14.829
companies. This was a time when companies and individuals

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:16.899
bailed out governments.

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:17.919
[laughter]

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:22.790
And at the UN, the United States was over a billion dollars

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.310
in arrears on their dues to the UN, and the UN couldn't pay

00:07:25.839 --> 00:07:29.279
their peacekeepers. We're the biggest contributor and we

00:07:29.299 --> 00:07:32.600
were almost two years behind in, in our payments.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.819
And I, it, it drove me crazy cause I, I, I'm a student of

00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:41.180
history and I read a lot when I was younger; studied history

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:44.740
very carefully and I don't think we'd be here today without

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:47.540
the United Nations. I think the United Nations did, through

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.779
all the things that they didn't do, and it's not perfect.

00:07:51.799 --> 00:07:54.490
Our federal governments not perfect, the state governments

00:07:54.509 --> 00:08:00.839
not perfect, but, but it did, maybe it was luck, but we did

00:08:00.859 --> 00:08:04.300
prevent World War III. And if we had had a World War III,

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:07.089
particularly about the time of the Cuban Missile Crisis,

00:08:07.109 --> 00:08:13.379
which was the closest we came, we'd probably all be dead.

00:08:13.399 --> 00:08:16.740
And that's worth saving. You know, something that saves

00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:19.750
everybody is worth hanging on to.

00:08:19.769 --> 00:08:24.589
And I was trying to figure out how I could get the money to

00:08:24.609 --> 00:08:27.860
the UN, because all of a sudden I had been worth, I was

00:08:27.879 --> 00:08:31.339
worth a couple billion dollars. And it happened real quickly

00:08:31.359 --> 00:08:35.429
when my stock went up when CNN turned a corner, in a period

00:08:35.449 --> 00:08:39.820
of just a few years, I went from just about broke to worth,

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.519
well, at, at the high point close to ten billion. And so, I

00:08:45.539 --> 00:08:48.789
had, I had the money and I, the first thing I thought is,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:52.740
is, "Why don't I go to the UN and buy the debt at, at a

00:08:52.759 --> 00:08:54.759
discount, you know." Offered at business school, right?

00:08:54.759 --> 00:08:55.759
[laughter]

00:08:54.759 --> 00:09:00.939
Because, and, and, and then I could, I could present the

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:04.320
bill to be paid. The UN couldn't push the United States

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.340
because they wanted them to stay a member, but they had to

00:09:07.259 --> 00:09:11.419
be real nice even though, if you owe somebody a lot of money

00:09:11.439 --> 00:09:14.689
you're supposed to be nice to them anyway. That's what get,

00:09:14.189 --> 00:09:17.439
forget about China. We've got to be nice to China because we

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:18.439
own 'em a trillion dollars.

00:09:18.439 --> 00:09:19.439
[laughter]

00:09:18.439 --> 00:09:21.689
Right? An idea of how we can get rid of that, too, real

00:09:21.709 --> 00:09:25.500
quickly is, is, you know China wants to build an aircraft

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:28.769
carrier because they'd like to, they've got a lot of money

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:31.649
now and they don't know what to do with some of it.

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:33.899
Aircraft carriers aren't much help in a place like

00:09:33.649 --> 00:09:36.649
Afghanistan, and we've got, I don't know, like 12 battle

00:09:36.589 --> 00:09:40.350
groups and we owe China a trillion dollars. Why don't we sit

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:42.929
down with them and say, "Look, you guys want aircraft

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:46.189
carriers. We can't use them in Afghanistan and Iraq in all

00:09:46.209 --> 00:09:49.120
the wars we're fighting, they weren't very helpful in

00:09:49.139 --> 00:09:54.129
Vietnam, either. We'll sell you, we'll give you our aircraft

00:09:54.149 --> 00:09:57.470
carriers if you write off the debt and then you can have a

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.490
Pacific fleet, you know?" What are you gonna do with it, you

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.240
know? I really think war is totally obsolete. That's, but

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.240
anyway, I was gonna buy--

00:10:03.589 --> 00:10:04.589
[laughter]

00:10:04.589 --> 00:10:07.850
I was gonna buy the debt and then sue the United States for

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.649
the full face amount, pick up a couple hundred million

00:10:10.669 --> 00:10:16.700
dollars on, on, on the deal. And I check with my lawyers and

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:19.970
they said, "You can't do that. An individual can't bring a

00:10:19.429 --> 00:10:25.799
lawsuit against the United States for unpaid bills." Ha!

00:10:25.819 --> 00:10:26.819
[laughter]

00:10:26.819 --> 00:10:27.819
So I--

00:10:27.199 --> 00:10:28.199
[laughter]

00:10:27.199 --> 00:10:33.139
That didn't work and it took me, it took me awhile because I

00:10:33.159 --> 00:10:37.240
kept thinking I, when I, it's like CNN. I'd, it, I'd spent

00:10:37.259 --> 00:10:41.389
three years just thinking about it before I even started it.

00:10:41.409 --> 00:10:46.939
I was just amazed that CBS, NBC, or, or CBS didn't, didn't

00:10:46.959 --> 00:10:49.459
do it cause they had all the infrastructure, the news

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.620
department, the bureaus, all they had to do was, was do it

00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:57.250
but they didn't have the imagination. And that's another

00:10:57.269 --> 00:11:00.519
good thing to do is go into a business where the people that

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:02.459
are running it are a bunch of dumbasses.

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:03.459
[laughter]

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:08.990
That makes it easy to have dumb competitors. It's one of the

00:11:09.009 --> 00:11:10.259
best ways to get started.

00:11:10.259 --> 00:11:11.259
[laughter]

00:11:10.259 --> 00:11:14.779
&gt;&gt;Jason: So when you think about the UN Foundation today,

00:11:14.799 --> 00:11:18.399
what are the most critical issues that it's working on and

00:11:18.419 --> 00:11:21.419
what are you most proud of in terms of what they've

00:11:20.919 --> 00:11:21.919
accomplished so far?

00:11:21.919 --> 00:11:24.669
&gt;&gt;Ted: Well, the thing that, that, that we're working on

00:11:24.419 --> 00:11:28.259
the, the most is trying to eradicate the intractable

00:11:28.279 --> 00:11:32.129
diseases that we, well they're not intractable because they,

00:11:32.149 --> 00:11:34.899
and, and, and we've done it with partnerships with, with

00:11:34.559 --> 00:11:36.870
Gates, with Rotary, for instance, with polio.

00:11:36.889 --> 00:11:42.250
We are maybe one year away, thanks to some breaks that we

00:11:42.269 --> 00:11:46.779
got, from eradicating polio. And that will only be the

00:11:46.799 --> 00:11:49.799
second disease in the history of the world that's ever been

00:11:49.549 --> 00:11:51.870
eradicated; po-, smallpox being the other one.

00:11:51.889 --> 00:11:55.139
That will be a gigantic win for those of you that are sports

00:11:54.939 --> 00:11:58.289
fans, which is just about everybody, you know, it's like, we

00:11:58.309 --> 00:12:03.289
need a Dubya. Humanity needs a Dubya. One, the last week,

00:12:03.309 --> 00:12:08.370
somebody blew up refugees were standing in line to get a

00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:13.699
little food in, in bowls and somebody killed 35 of 'em. What

00:12:13.719 --> 00:12:18.500
in the hell are you bombing refugees for? I mean, who, what,

00:12:18.519 --> 00:12:20.340
what does a-, accomplish?

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:23.250
What, are they a different religion? What, in the name of

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:27.090
God, are we doing? Why don't we start acting like civilized,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:32.230
educated, decent, kindhearted human beings? That's, why don'

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.750
t we start doing that? It's not that hard. I spent my whole

00:12:35.679 --> 00:12:37.929
life doing it and it's been a ball.

00:12:37.699 --> 00:12:38.699
[laughter]

00:12:37.699 --> 00:12:41.309
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, you've spoken--

00:12:41.329 --> 00:12:45.370
&gt;&gt;Ted: Most people like to help other people. Like in Haiti,

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:48.919
we spent a million dollars the first day, you know? And, and

00:12:48.939 --> 00:12:51.740
millions and millions of dollars, most people wanna help

00:12:51.759 --> 00:12:54.860
other people. Who in this room would like to bomb somebody?

00:12:54.879 --> 00:12:58.429
Not one person. How many people wanna help other people?

00:12:58.449 --> 00:13:02.629
Yeah, that's right, that's, that's humanity. That's, why

00:13:02.649 --> 00:13:04.649
don't we start acting like human beings?

00:13:04.319 --> 00:13:08.269
&gt;&gt;Jason: One of the topics you've spoken out a lot about and

00:13:08.289 --> 00:13:10.789
I think you're most passionate about is climate change.

00:13:10.539 --> 00:13:13.960
&gt;&gt;Ted: One of 'em cause it, it poses a real threat to us.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.730
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, if you were running this country what are the

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:19.230
three things you would do to help combat this issue?

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:20.230
&gt;&gt;Ted: Well, why three?

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:21.230
[laughter]

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:29.470
Isn't that awfully arbitrary?

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:30.490
[laughter and clapping]

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.740
&gt;&gt;Jason: Ok, how about seven things you'd do?

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:34.490
&gt;&gt;Ted: That's like my competitors at CBS, ABC, and NBC. They

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:37.240
only thought of three, but I thought of ten ways to attack '

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:37.990
em and they--

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:38.990
[laughter]

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:41.240
they stopped me on the first three, but I got 'em with the

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:41.740
other seven.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:42.740
[laughter]

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.590
&gt;&gt;Jason: You didn't answer the question, though, so don't--

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:45.610
[laughter]

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:55.139
&gt;&gt;Ted: Because it was such a dumb question.

00:13:55.159 --> 00:13:56.159
[laughter and clapping]

00:13:56.159 --> 00:14:00.269
[ ] Well, one I'd ask people to turn out the lights. You

00:14:00.289 --> 00:14:05.069
know, basically the, the, the, the cheapest and, and, and

00:14:05.089 --> 00:14:09.529
quickest way to make progress is to, is to conserve.

00:14:09.549 --> 00:14:13.039
Conserve energy. Walk, don't drive and you know.

00:14:13.059 --> 00:14:17.139
So, that, that, that sort of thing and then, and what we

00:14:17.159 --> 00:14:20.860
have to do is switch over to, it's time to say goodbye to

00:14:20.879 --> 00:14:25.740
coal and oil, and replace it with renewable energy like

00:14:25.759 --> 00:14:29.769
solar, wind, and possibly geothermal, if we can figure,

00:14:29.789 --> 00:14:31.610
figure out how to, to do it.

00:14:31.629 --> 00:14:34.879
We should be doing a lot of research on it because it has

00:14:34.879 --> 00:14:37.889
tremendous potential. And it?s time to, to, the Industrial

00:14:37.909 --> 00:14:41.259
Revolution was started with burning coal, its time; it's

00:14:41.279 --> 00:14:44.779
time to say goodbye to it. We're burning too much of it and

00:14:44.259 --> 00:14:47.759
it's, and it's poisonous. And we're gonna, we're gonna run

00:14:47.189 --> 00:14:51.909
out of it anyway, but, but it's time to say goodbye to coal.

00:14:51.929 --> 00:14:54.439
And I mean say goodbye to coal.

00:14:54.459 --> 00:14:58.490
Those 35 senators from the coal states, they're gonna vote

00:14:58.509 --> 00:15:01.990
for coal. They don't care what happens, they do have care

00:15:02.009 --> 00:15:04.539
what happens to the country, but they care more about

00:15:04.559 --> 00:15:07.360
whether they get re-elected than anything else. We're gonna

00:15:07.379 --> 00:15:12.579
have to get our government to work better. And, because it,

00:15:12.599 --> 00:15:16.349
then they'd have to be able to do more than one thing at a

00:15:16.099 --> 00:15:19.349
time. I mean, I ran CNN and the Cartoon Network at the same

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.110
time.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:21.110
[laughter]

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:26.399
And, we have to multitask. I mean, they, they stop. The

00:15:26.419 --> 00:15:29.539
whole country comes to a halt for six months while they get

00:15:29.559 --> 00:15:32.699
the, get the healthcare bill through. Why can't they do two

00:15:32.719 --> 00:15:35.969
or three bills at one time, you know? And I mean you take

00:15:35.939 --> 00:15:37.689
more than one course, don't you?

00:15:37.439 --> 00:15:38.439
[laughter]

00:15:37.439 --> 00:15:44.679
For Christ's sake, I mean, you know.

00:15:44.699 --> 00:15:45.699
[laughter]

00:15:44.699 --> 00:15:46.199
You gotta get with the program.

00:15:46.179 --> 00:15:47.179
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, you mean--

00:15:47.179 --> 00:15:49.679
&gt;&gt;Ted: Speed things up a bit. Go into high gear.

00:15:49.629 --> 00:15:50.629
&gt;&gt;Jason: I like that.

00:15:50.629 --> 00:15:51.629
[laughter]

00:15:50.629 --> 00:15:54.689
See, you mentioned China before and I think one of the big

00:15:54.709 --> 00:15:57.459
challenges is with addressing climate change is it's really

00:15:57.479 --> 00:16:00.730
a global issue and countries like China and India are

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:03.139
projected to be the biggest producers of carbon emissions

00:16:03.159 --> 00:16:04.159
over the next decade.

00:16:04.159 --> 00:16:07.480
&gt;&gt;Ted: You know China is very, very close to it right now.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.500
&gt;&gt;Jason: So what should the US be doing to figure out the

00:16:10.289 --> 00:16:11.289
global dynamics here?

00:16:11.289 --> 00:16:12.289
[pause]

00:16:11.289 --> 00:16:18.019
&gt;&gt;Ted: Basically, there's a lot of people thinking of it,

00:16:18.039 --> 00:16:24.629
thinking about it and, and, and working on it. I, I am and

00:16:24.649 --> 00:16:28.769
it's, we just gotta, we gotta, we can't really afford to

00:16:28.789 --> 00:16:32.179
lose this one. And it's not just climate change. Climate

00:16:32.199 --> 00:16:36.139
change is a symptom of overpopulation and too many people

00:16:36.159 --> 00:16:40.529
uses too much stuff. If we, what we have to do is we gotta

00:16:40.549 --> 00:16:43.199
stabilize the population.

00:16:43.219 --> 00:16:46.389
And, and, and, and, and, and, and quickly because we can't

00:16:46.409 --> 00:16:49.649
handle nine billion people, well, 6.7 billion. When I was

00:16:49.669 --> 00:16:53.039
born in 1938, there were only two billion people in the

00:16:53.059 --> 00:16:56.730
world. In, in, in, in the lifetime of one person, in those

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.389
70 years, we've increased the number of people on the planet

00:17:00.409 --> 00:17:01.909
by three and a half times.

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:05.619
That's, that's gigantic. Nothing like that ever happened

00:17:05.639 --> 00:17:08.389
before. If we'd have got that many more elephants, for

00:17:08.139 --> 00:17:11.750
Christ's sake, we'd all be elephant dung, you know?

00:17:11.769 --> 00:17:12.769
[laughter]

00:17:12.769 --> 00:17:16.519
So, we've got to, we've got to go, what we really need to

00:17:16.119 --> 00:17:19.619
do, and Paul Ehrlich, I don't know if he's still around, but

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:22.839
he's, he's involved here I think, I, I read The Population

00:17:22.399 --> 00:17:27.569
Bomb 30 years ago. And he, he, he feels like that, cause I

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:31.289
talked to him, I had him out for a weekend 30 years ago when

00:17:31.309 --> 00:17:35.829
I was doing my research on population. And he thought that

00:17:35.849 --> 00:17:38.849
the world population ought to be between two and two and a

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:40.599
half billion people.

00:17:40.619 --> 00:17:44.609
With that, with the support, you know, internal combustion

00:17:44.629 --> 00:17:47.829
engines, there wouldn't be so many people that would

00:17:47.849 --> 00:17:51.670
overwhelm the, the, the resource base. The planet's

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.950
collapsing all around us. Ocean fisheries are collapsing

00:17:54.970 --> 00:18:01.329
from overfishing, the top soil, the top soils eroding away

00:18:01.349 --> 00:18:04.549
from farming; the kind of farming that we do where we plow

00:18:04.569 --> 00:18:09.519
and, and wind is, and water erosion are washing the, are

00:18:09.539 --> 00:18:11.009
washing the top soil away.

00:18:11.029 --> 00:18:16.139
We gotta take better care of the planet because without,

00:18:16.159 --> 00:18:20.529
without a healthy environment, it's, the populations not

00:18:20.549 --> 00:18:24.329
gonna make it. And with it, and with that 6.7 billion

00:18:24.349 --> 00:18:27.970
population, there's a billion people going to bed hungry at

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.240
night. What are we gonna do if we had two or three billion

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.490
more people? And that's where we're headed if we keep going,

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:37.490
going the way that we're going. So, we have to do that first

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:41.319
because that's the most important thing. And, and, we, or we

00:18:41.339 --> 00:18:42.589
have to do it simultaneously.

00:18:42.589 --> 00:18:45.839
So we're gonna have to multitask. We're gonna have to stop

00:18:45.339 --> 00:18:48.180
doing the dumb things and start doing the smart things that

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:52.269
we gotta start with you. You're generation has got to be the

00:18:52.289 --> 00:18:55.049
one that does it because if you wait till another generation

00:18:55.069 --> 00:18:57.819
before you start turning things around, it's gonna be too

00:18:57.789 --> 00:19:03.659
late. So, and global warming and climate change is just one

00:19:03.679 --> 00:19:06.659
symptom of a sick planet because of too many people using

00:19:06.679 --> 00:19:07.679
too much stuff.

00:19:07.679 --> 00:19:12.359
&gt;&gt;Jason: Going back to business for a second, over the last

00:19:12.379 --> 00:19:15.700
ten years business, business has gotten a bad rap. From

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.299
Enron to the recent financial crisis--

00:19:17.319 --> 00:19:19.069
&gt;&gt;Ted: A lot of it was deserved.

00:19:19.069 --> 00:19:21.569
&gt;&gt;Jason: I was gonna say, business leaders are more vilified

00:19:21.569 --> 00:19:24.619
than glorified. What do you think the reason is for that and

00:19:24.639 --> 00:19:26.389
what can we do to change that?

00:19:26.389 --> 00:19:27.639
&gt;&gt;Ted: Cause they're crooks.

00:19:27.389 --> 00:19:28.389
[laughter]

00:19:27.389 --> 00:19:31.539
They deserve being vilified. You know, just like the good

00:19:31.559 --> 00:19:36.119
guys outta be lionized, like me.

00:19:36.139 --> 00:19:37.139
[laughter]

00:19:37.139 --> 00:19:40.490
I never did anything wrong. Not anything substantially

00:19:40.509 --> 00:19:42.279
wrong.

00:19:42.299 --> 00:19:43.299
[laughter]

00:19:42.299 --> 00:19:45.399
Not that I got caught.

00:19:45.419 --> 00:19:46.419
[laughter]

00:19:45.419 --> 00:19:48.169
Well, I would've gotten caught, too, because I was high

00:19:48.019 --> 00:19:50.700
profile enough and they were, enough people would've liked

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:54.819
me. We need, you gotta play by the rules. You've got to, and

00:19:54.839 --> 00:19:57.839
it's not that hard. You could, you can make billions doing

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:00.750
it playing by the rules and doing it honestly. If you're

00:20:00.769 --> 00:20:02.430
smart enough and work hard enough.

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:05.899
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, on the topic of the financial crisis,

00:20:05.919 --> 00:20:09.549
reportedly in 2007, you transferred almost all of your

00:20:09.569 --> 00:20:12.279
financial portfolio into cash, successfully anticipating the

00:20:12.299 --> 00:20:13.299
crisis.

00:20:13.299 --> 00:20:14.529
&gt;&gt;Ted: Bonds, right.

00:20:14.549 --> 00:20:15.549
&gt;&gt;Jason: Ok, or bonds.

00:20:15.549 --> 00:20:19.049
&gt;&gt;Ted: But that's, that wasn't the reason I did it. I had--

00:20:18.549 --> 00:20:21.299
&gt;&gt;Jason: So I was gonna ask you, what are the reasons?

00:20:21.299 --> 00:20:24.299
&gt;&gt;Ted: I had lost between seven and eight billion on the AOL

00:20:24.299 --> 00:20:27.049
transaction, and gone from eight or nine billion and for a

00:20:27.049 --> 00:20:31.319
brief moment, I think a high, I went over ten. And I was

00:20:31.339 --> 00:20:36.180
back down to under two and I was just, and that was in Time

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:40.720
Warner. I thought, you know, I'd, I was, by cashing in my

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:46.930
chips when I merged with Time Warner and, and, and, and,

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.240
but, but I lost so much in

00:20:49.259 --> 00:20:52.500
[ ] with AOL, it was just hard to believe we could make so

00:20:52.519 --> 00:20:53.519
many mistakes.

00:20:53.519 --> 00:20:56.950
It was really just one. Merging with them without doing

00:20:56.970 --> 00:21:00.109
adequate due diligence and finding out that the books were

00:21:00.129 --> 00:21:03.379
cooked and we didn't know it and the auditors didn't catch

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:06.099
it, but, of course, we only did one day of due diligence.

00:21:06.119 --> 00:21:07.119
[laughter]

00:21:07.119 --> 00:21:11.519
Anyway, yeah, it's on a forty billion dollar deal, or

00:21:11.539 --> 00:21:14.200
hundred billion dollar deal, depending on what you're

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:18.829
grossing up. So, I, I just wanted to hang onto, there's

00:21:18.849 --> 00:21:26.119
nothing worse than being a broke, old man who once was rich.

00:21:26.139 --> 00:21:27.139
[laughter]

00:21:26.139 --> 00:21:30.259
And I wanted to be able to have enough money to cover my

00:21:30.279 --> 00:21:31.279
funeral--

00:21:31.279 --> 00:21:32.279
[laughter]

00:21:31.589 --> 00:21:36.549
without having my children having to cough it up.

00:21:36.569 --> 00:21:39.569
&gt;&gt;Jason: Do you think there are steps we should've taken to

00:21:39.579 --> 00:21:40.579
prevent the financial crisis?

00:21:40.579 --> 00:21:45.950
&gt;&gt;Ted: Yeah. We should not have borrowed so much money. I

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:49.970
don't owe a penny to a soul and haven't for a long time. I

00:21:49.539 --> 00:21:52.889
think, I think you outta aim in your business career and

00:21:52.909 --> 00:21:57.259
your life to be out of debt by the time you're 60. And not

00:21:57.279 --> 00:21:59.419
owe anything on your house or anything else.

00:21:59.439 --> 00:22:02.689
That and the reason for that is because after you're 60, it'

00:22:02.509 --> 00:22:06.419
s hard to find a job. People don't want you because of

00:22:06.439 --> 00:22:10.109
medical care, you know, you've got people, older people have

00:22:10.129 --> 00:22:13.240
more health problems than younger people and so forth. It's

00:22:13.259 --> 00:22:16.899
really hard, hard to find a job over 60, unless you want to

00:22:16.919 --> 00:22:18.919
be a greeter at Wal-Mart, you know.

00:22:18.369 --> 00:22:19.369
[audience chuckles]

00:22:19.369 --> 00:22:22.869
I know, because I haven't had a job in over ten years. That'

00:22:22.409 --> 00:22:25.409
s one reason I had to start these philanthropies and, I just

00:22:25.409 --> 00:22:28.909
had to have some kind of activity. So if I don't have it

00:22:28.659 --> 00:22:31.159
coming in, at least I can have it going out.

00:22:31.159 --> 00:22:32.159
[laughter]

00:22:31.159 --> 00:22:34.158
Movement, you know, just don't stand still. Do something.

00:22:34.178 --> 00:22:37.789
&gt;&gt;Jason: Are there any CEOs or business leaders today that

00:22:37.809 --> 00:22:38.809
you admire?

00:22:38.809 --> 00:22:44.099
&gt;&gt;Ted: Yeah. I, I, I like Warren Buffet. And I like, I like

00:22:44.119 --> 00:22:47.779
Bill Gates. I like the winners that, that--

00:22:47.799 --> 00:22:48.799
[chuckles]

00:22:47.799 --> 00:22:50.740
there's some other ones.

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:51.759
[pause]

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:51.759
[laughter]

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:58.069
&gt;&gt;Jason: So, I guess, you know, looking back you've founded

00:22:58.089 --> 00:23:00.589
a media empire, became a billionaire, started a restaurant

00:23:00.609 --> 00:23:01.609
chain--

00:23:01.609 --> 00:23:02.859
&gt;&gt;Ted: Won the World Series.

00:23:02.619 --> 00:23:03.869
&gt;&gt;Jason: won the World Series, --

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.480
&gt;&gt;Ted: That was important.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:08.379
&gt;&gt;Jason: invested in renewable energy, married a movie star.

00:23:08.399 --> 00:23:11.009
For those people in the audience who aspire--

00:23:11.029 --> 00:23:12.029
[laughter]

00:23:12.029 --> 00:23:15.029
to do something like that, if you have any advice to us

00:23:14.799 --> 00:23:16.049
going out into the workforce.

00:23:16.049 --> 00:23:25.269
&gt;&gt;Ted: Pump a lot of iron. It's tiring. But I really had a

00:23:25.289 --> 00:23:29.289
great time. I've, I, I, I, I've had a great life and I'm

00:23:28.839 --> 00:23:30.339
having a great life right now.

00:23:29.839 --> 00:23:30.839
&gt;&gt;Jason: Right.

00:23:30.839 --> 00:23:31.839
&gt;&gt;Ted: I really am.

00:23:31.839 --> 00:23:34.589
&gt;&gt;Jason: Well, before we turn it over to the audience here

00:23:34.589 --> 00:23:38.359
for some questions, in 2008, you celebrated your 70th

00:23:38.379 --> 00:23:41.419
birthday with a party in Atlanta and reportedly, you spent

00:23:41.439 --> 00:23:43.690
some time reading poetry to the guests and--

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:46.460
&gt;&gt;Ted: No, I didn't. I, I quoted it from memory.

00:23:46.339 --> 00:23:49.769
&gt;&gt;Jason: Even better. So you said at the time, that the

00:23:49.789 --> 00:23:51.789
words that have meant the most to you--

00:23:51.789 --> 00:23:53.539
&gt;&gt;Ted: I would do it again, too.

00:23:53.539 --> 00:23:55.789
&gt;&gt;Jason: are integrity from Shakespeare. Would you like to

00:23:55.789 --> 00:23:57.289
share some of that with us?

00:23:57.289 --> 00:24:00.289
&gt;&gt;Ted: I think its Richard the Third. It's short. The best

00:24:00.039 --> 00:24:07.589
lines on integrity that I've ever, that I've ever heard. "My

00:24:07.609 --> 00:24:13.149
honor is life, we live in one. Take honor from me, and my

00:24:13.169 --> 00:24:19.809
life is done. Then pray, my liege, my honor let me try for

00:24:19.829 --> 00:24:23.220
that I live, and for that will I die."

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.069
&gt;&gt;Jason: Thank you very much.

00:24:26.089 --> 00:24:27.089
[applause]

00:24:26.089 --> 00:24:37.559
&gt;&gt;Ted: I wanna give you the other two that I gave at my

00:24:37.579 --> 00:24:40.079
birthday, too; the lines that, that on courage. Because if

00:24:40.079 --> 00:24:43.079
you're gonna be successful in business you gotta have a lot

00:24:42.829 --> 00:24:48.389
of courage. "Then stepped forward, Horatius." This is from

00:24:48.409 --> 00:24:52.970
Horatius at the Bridge by Thomas McCauley. "Then step

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:57.169
forward, Horatuis, Captain of the Gate. He said, 'To every

00:24:57.189 --> 00:25:01.960
man of woman born, death cometh soon or late.' And how can

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:06.990
man die better than facing fearful odds, for the ashes of

00:25:07.009 --> 00:25:11.409
his fathers and the temples of his Gods. Hew down the

00:25:11.429 --> 00:25:16.329
bridge, Sir Consul, with all the speed ye may; I, but with

00:25:16.349 --> 00:25:21.429
two beside me will hold the foe in play. In yon narrow span

00:25:21.449 --> 00:25:25.339
a thousand, might well be stopped by three. Now who will

00:25:25.359 --> 00:25:28.690
stand in either hand, and guard the bridge with me?"

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:31.709
Pretty strong, it stopped the whole

00:25:31.729 --> 00:25:31.929
[ ]

00:25:31.929 --> 00:25:32.929
[applause]

00:25:31.929 --> 00:25:42.549
And the last one is the longest one and I really, I hope I

00:25:42.569 --> 00:25:44.740
don't forget a line and, but

00:25:44.759 --> 00:25:47.759
[ ]. And it's also about Richard the Third and it's about

00:25:47.579 --> 00:25:50.409
humility.

00:25:50.429 --> 00:25:53.309
"No matter where; of comfort no man speak:

00:25:53.329 --> 00:25:57.549
Let's talk of worms, of graves, of epitaphs;

00:25:57.569 --> 00:26:00.159
Make dust our paper and with rainy eyes

00:26:00.179 --> 00:26:02.889
Write sorrow on the bosom of earth.

00:26:02.909 --> 00:26:05.960
Let's choose executors and talk of wills:

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:08.440
And yet not so, for what can we bequeath

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:12.549
Save our deposed bodies to the ground?

00:26:12.569 --> 00:26:15.960
Our lands, our lives and all are Bolingbroke's,

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:19.250
And nothing can we call our own save death

00:26:19.269 --> 00:26:21.679
And that small model of the barren earth

00:26:21.699 --> 00:26:25.220
That serves as paste and cover to our bones.

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:28.589
For God's sake, let us sit upon the ground

00:26:28.609 --> 00:26:32.079
And tell sad stories of the death of kings;

00:26:32.099 --> 00:26:35.619
How some have been deposed; some slain in war,

00:26:35.639 --> 00:26:38.750
Some haunted by the ghosts they have de-, deposed;

00:26:38.769 --> 00:26:42.879
Some poisoned by their wives: some sleeping killed;

00:26:42.899 --> 00:26:46.259
All murdered: for within the hollow crown

00:26:46.279 --> 00:26:48.980
That rounds the mortal temples of a king

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:52.429
Keeps Death his watch and there the antic sits,

00:26:52.449 --> 00:26:56.099
Scoffing at his state and grinning at his pomp,

00:26:56.119 --> 00:26:58.980
Infusing him with self and vain conceit,

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:02.089
As if this flesh which walls about our life,

00:27:02.109 --> 00:27:04.119
Were brass impregnable, and

00:27:04.139 --> 00:27:06.779
Comes at the last and with a little pin

00:27:06.799 --> 00:27:11.539
Bores through his castle wall, and farewell king!

00:27:11.559 --> 00:27:14.139
Cover your heads and mock not flesh and blood

00:27:14.159 --> 00:27:17.799
With solemn reverence: throw away respect,

00:27:17.819 --> 00:27:22.720
Tradition, form and ceremonious duty,

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:26.899
I live with bread like you, feel want,

00:27:26.919 --> 00:27:32.339
Taste grief, need friends: subjected thus,

00:27:32.359 --> 00:27:35.509
How can you say to me, I am a king?"

00:27:35.529 --> 00:27:36.529
[applause]

00:27:35.529 --> 00:27:56.849
It's what, my company, in 1970, the first year in the

00:27:56.869 --> 00:28:03.059
television business, there were 35 employees and we did six

00:28:03.079 --> 00:28:05.980
hundred thousand dollars and lost nine hundred thousand;

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:11.829
almost a million. That same exact company, 30 years later,

00:28:11.849 --> 00:28:17.819
did 2.5 billion, had 12,000 employees and made six hundred

00:28:17.839 --> 00:28:21.139
million. That was the year I merged with Time Warner. That,

00:28:21.159 --> 00:28:24.899
that's what you could do, just say, "Smoke 'em." The stock

00:28:24.919 --> 00:28:28.519
went up 2,000 for one during that time and that's how you

00:28:28.539 --> 00:28:32.230
make 'em. You wanna be a billionaire, start with nothing and

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:33.500
turn it into a fortune.

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:34.500
[laughter and clapping]

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:45.139
&gt;&gt;Jason: So now, we'd like to open it up to questions from

00:28:45.159 --> 00:28:48.159
the audience. Please take a mic and make sure to stand up.

00:28:48.129 --> 00:28:53.679
&gt;&gt;audience1: Thanks very much for coming today. I wanted to

00:28:53.699 --> 00:28:57.119
ask you a question about mentorship. I'm wondering who have

00:28:57.139 --> 00:28:59.990
been some of your biggest mentors over your career and what

00:29:00.009 --> 00:29:02.759
are some of the biggest lessons you've learned from them?

00:29:01.809 --> 00:29:06.319
&gt;&gt;Ted: My father was definitely the biggest one. He taught

00:29:06.339 --> 00:29:10.829
me about business. It was, he was in the billboard business,

00:29:10.849 --> 00:29:14.099
it was a real easy business. You lease the space, put up a

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:17.750
sign, and then rented it to an advertiser for enough to have

00:29:17.769 --> 00:29:18.829
a profit.

00:29:18.849 --> 00:29:22.629
But, I wanted to do more with my life than just make money,

00:29:22.649 --> 00:29:26.179
so I wanted to get to television so I could do programming.

00:29:26.199 --> 00:29:30.819
I wanted, I had ideas for, for programs and so that's what

00:29:30.839 --> 00:29:34.460
I, but he was, he taught me about how to, you know, what

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:36.309
profits and losses were and so forth.

00:29:36.329 --> 00:29:37.329
[pause]

00:29:36.329 --> 00:29:41.869
&gt;&gt;audience2: Mr. Turner, Ted,--

00:29:41.889 --> 00:29:43.279
&gt;&gt;Ted: Ted.

00:29:43.299 --> 00:29:44.299
[laughter]

00:29:43.299 --> 00:29:45.549
&gt;&gt;audience2: Thank you for joining us today. During this

00:29:45.419 --> 00:29:48.450
conversation, we talked about both your experience as an

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:50.980
entrepreneur, fighting against the odds, and also for your

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:53.689
passion about the environment; a lot of the challenges that

00:29:53.709 --> 00:29:56.019
even people here at Stanford have characterized in our

00:29:56.039 --> 00:29:57.899
global ecosystem.

00:29:57.919 --> 00:30:00.919
I was wondering if you could take a pass at bringing those

00:30:00.759 --> 00:30:04.490
two together and speak to one, to what extent, what advice

00:30:04.509 --> 00:30:07.509
you have for leaders who want to have an impact in those

00:30:07.149 --> 00:30:10.619
issues, and two, do you have any specific, are there any

00:30:10.639 --> 00:30:13.289
specific opportunities that excite you outside of energy,

00:30:13.309 --> 00:30:16.009
whether it be water or soil, agriculture, etc.?

00:30:16.029 --> 00:30:24.379
&gt;&gt;Ted: Well, I think of, I, I think of soil and, and water

00:30:24.399 --> 00:30:29.419
and energy to-, to-, together, but, but I think that's,

00:30:29.439 --> 00:30:33.220
that's where I would, if I was starting a business today,

00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:36.740
that's, that's where I'd go because we've got to completely

00:30:35.799 --> 00:30:40.559
rebuild our whole energy system all over the world. We've

00:30:40.579 --> 00:30:44.399
got to save that coal and, and oil.

00:30:44.419 --> 00:30:48.169
We've, I, I, I'm, I'm going along with Boone Pickens that we

00:30:47.859 --> 00:30:53.359
outta use natural gas as a bridge fuel to, when we're

00:30:53.379 --> 00:30:59.079
completely reliant on, on solar wind and, and perhaps some

00:30:59.099 --> 00:31:06.849
geothermal and, and bio, biofuels. That, that's where,

00:31:06.869 --> 00:31:10.119
where, where I'd go. I think that's where the, the growth

00:31:09.629 --> 00:31:13.319
industries for the, for the future are. At least for the

00:31:13.339 --> 00:31:20.679
immediate future; for the next thirty forty years.

00:31:20.699 --> 00:31:24.419
And I'm in; I'm personally going there myself. I, we're

00:31:24.439 --> 00:31:27.740
building one of the largest solar installations in New

00:31:27.759 --> 00:31:31.129
Mexico with, I partnered with the Southern Company, which is

00:31:31.149 --> 00:31:34.230
a big utility; the biggest one in the Southeast, one of the

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:37.129
biggest ones in the country. And they are, half of their

00:31:37.149 --> 00:31:42.389
power is generated from coal and, but what I'm doing is I'm

00:31:42.409 --> 00:31:45.759
partnering up with them and trying to work with them and to

00:31:45.779 --> 00:31:49.230
show them that we can go, that they can go with clean energy

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:54.899
rather than fight them. But, but I've been known to do a

00:31:54.919 --> 00:31:57.490
little fighting, too. From time to time.

00:31:57.509 --> 00:31:58.509
[chuckles from audience]

00:31:57.509 --> 00:31:58.509
Yes, sir?

00:31:58.009 --> 00:32:01.199
&gt;&gt;audience3: Thanks for coming. I'm Steven

00:32:01.219 --> 00:32:06.529
[ ], I'm a first year student here at the GSB. So, a lot of

00:32:06.549 --> 00:32:09.299
the ide-, the business ideas that you've spoken about have

00:32:09.049 --> 00:32:12.519
been, you know, business ideas with a social mission. Media

00:32:12.539 --> 00:32:15.710
in itself is effectively a business i-, is a business that

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:18.730
does have a large social mission and I think that is around

00:32:18.659 --> 00:32:19.679
education.

00:32:19.699 --> 00:32:22.699
I wonder if you could talk a little bit about getting the

00:32:22.349 --> 00:32:26.730
right trade-off between propaganda and education and how

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:30.449
much mass appeal you get versus, you know, a smaller, a

00:32:30.469 --> 00:32:33.269
smaller audience and how you and, you and CNN broadly have

00:32:33.289 --> 00:32:36.139
struggled with that. Well, not necessarily you struggled

00:32:36.159 --> 00:32:38.730
with that, but how you found that, that trade-off.

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:42.139
&gt;&gt;Ted: I'm gonna ask, cause I'm hard of hearing, the-, the-,

00:32:42.159 --> 00:32:45.439
the-, there were about three or four questions, there.

00:32:45.459 --> 00:32:46.459
[laughter]

00:32:45.459 --> 00:32:48.759
That's very complex. I mean, it take a, a,

00:32:48.779 --> 00:32:54.049
[ ] That's a good question and you have to, as far as, as

00:32:54.069 --> 00:33:00.019
far as, just take the news media, take the news network,

00:33:00.039 --> 00:33:06.059
take CNN. They're being beaten in the ratings by Fox, which

00:33:06.079 --> 00:33:09.179
I knew that that was where the greatest threat to CNN would

00:33:09.199 --> 00:33:12.199
come from. It'd come from a right-wing network. This was

00:33:11.639 --> 00:33:14.049
before I even started at CNN.

00:33:14.069 --> 00:33:17.689
That they're, because, and, and, and they have done a good

00:33:17.709 --> 00:33:21.299
job of filling that niche. On the one hand, you could say

00:33:21.319 --> 00:33:25.289
that they're not as responsible as I would like to see them,

00:33:25.309 --> 00:33:28.849
see them be, but they're very successful. A, and, and

00:33:28.869 --> 00:33:32.789
sometimes doing the wrong thing is more successful than

00:33:32.809 --> 00:33:36.889
doing the right thing. And, and that's one of the hardest

00:33:36.909 --> 00:33:40.769
things about business is I, I, I couldn't do the wrong thing

00:33:40.789 --> 00:33:44.939
and make money on it. I, I, I just, I have too much pride to

00:33:44.959 --> 00:33:46.009
do that.

00:33:46.029 --> 00:33:50.389
As, I'd rather not be successful than be successful by doing

00:33:50.409 --> 00:33:56.539
the wrong thing. And for me, it worked. Luckily. It doesn't

00:33:56.559 --> 00:34:01.899
work for everybody and it, it's, it's a very hard thing to,

00:34:01.919 --> 00:34:06.059
to balance. It, it really is. And that, and that, you know,

00:34:06.079 --> 00:34:09.329
it's one thing just to go into business to make some money

00:34:08.779 --> 00:34:11.779
but most people have enough trouble. That's hard to do by

00:34:11.779 --> 00:34:14.980
itself. And then if you have strong so-, sense of social

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.279
consciousness on top of that, that's just is another layer

00:34:18.299 --> 00:34:19.819
of complexity to it.

00:34:19.839 --> 00:34:23.610
But I would strongly recommend, because I did it and was

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:29.340
successful that way, that, that you go strive that way to,

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:33.099
to, to, to do it with a, with a conscious; that what you do

00:34:33.119 --> 00:34:38.630
is something you can be proud of. And I, because I think in

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:42.650
the long run, that's really more important that you have

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:47.409
your pride and, and you did good with your life rather than

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:53.599
just making a whole lot of money. But it's not easy.

00:34:53.619 --> 00:34:58.090
&gt;&gt;audience4: Hi, Ted. My name is Ebert Casano. I grew up

